  "problem": "Compute $P(-1<X\\le 0)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}(x+2)(1-x),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1<X\\le 0) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{2}{9}(x+2)(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{[-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}([-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+2(0)]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+2(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(0-[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-2]) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
